For Newton these active (and passive) principles were God’s intermediaries 
in his governance of his Creation. They were not intrinsic to matter, but partici- 
pated in the spiritual realm of Christ’s vice-regency in the world, and thereby 
constituted for Newton a sign of God’s existence and a proof of His manage- 
ment of the world of the natural philosopher. All of which was in keeping with 
Newton’s view of the scope of natural philosophy: ‘And thus much concerning 
God; to discourse of whom from the appearances of things, does certainly 
belong to Natural Philosophy’.*? 


