underlying weakness. It is no coincidence that discussions of the supposed 
decline of science in nineteenth-century France, have tended to draw their evi- 
dence from the contrast between the golden age of mathematical physics in the 
age of Laplace and the period from the mid-nineteenth century in which 
French contributions ceased to lead the field. 


